   "name": "Radiation and emissions into the atmosphere"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Radiation is energy that travels as waves: light, infrared (heat), ultraviolet (UV), radio waves, X-rays.\nHuman activities emit (send out) harmful substances into the atmosphere: carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide from burning fuel and bushes, smoke and soot, gases from fridges and aerosol sprays (CFCs, which damage the ozone layer), dust.\nThe Earth itself gives out infrared radiation (heat). Gases like carbon dioxide trap some of it.\nEffects: air pollution, coughs and asthma, damage to the ozone layer, global warming. Reducing emissions: maintain engines, use clean energy, plant trees, stop bush fires.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Name two emissions from a bush fire and one effect.",
  "steps": [
   "Burning plants releases smoke (soot) and carbon dioxide.",
   "Effect: the air is polluted and people cough; carbon dioxide adds to global warming."
  ],
  "answer": "smoke and carbon dioxide; air pollution and warming",
  "checks": []
 },
